[38;5;231m⏺[39m Third fork: [1mhow the module is loaded and tested.[0m This matters because it's an interface everything later inherits, and
  the repo has no test runner or module convention to follow — [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS ([38;5;153mrequire[39m), while [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain
  browser script with no module system at all, and the two never talk to each other.

  - [1mES module + Node's built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m)[0m — one [38;5;153mpreferences.js[39m that browsers load natively via [38;5;153m<script [39m
    [38;5;153mtype="module">[39m and Node tests import directly. Zero dependencies, no build step, no config. Cost: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes a
    module too (a one-line change in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m), and it won't match the older CommonJS style in [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
  - [1mCommonJS to match [38;5;153msrc/[0m — consistent with the existing module code, but browsers can't load [38;5;153mrequire()[39m natively, so it
    needs either a bundler or a dual global/CommonJS wrapper. That's real complexity for a two-file app.
  - [1mPlain global script + a test shim[0m — no module system at all, matching [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as it stands. Simplest for the browser,
    but tests have to load the file by hand, and the global namespace is the interface.

  I recommend the ES module with [38;5;153mnode --test[39m. It's the only option that's natively correct in both the browser and the
  test runner with no dependencies and no build step, and the CommonJS code in [38;5;153msrc/[39m is unrelated demo code this feature
  never touches — consistency with it buys nothing.

  Alongside that, since the repo has no tooling configured at all, this is the cheapest moment to add some.
